  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote `src/main.ts`, which typechecks, but I couldn't build or run it in a browser here. The shaders have never been compiled and I haven't seen a rendered frame. A shader compile error would log `CUSTOM_SHADER_COMPILE_FAILED` and skip that draw silently.\n\n- **Water:** a custom GLSL material (`material.custom`) on a grid of 251×251 vertices. The vertices are packed densely near the camera and extend about 450 m to the horizon. The vertex shader displaces the grid with four sine waves and computes the normal analytically. The fragment shader adds noise ripples to that normal, fading with distance. It uses Schlick fresnel to mix the water body colour with the reflected sky, adds a sun glint, and blends far water into horizon haze.\n- **Sky:** a large inverted sphere with its own custom shader. It draws a gradient, a sun disc and noise clouds. The water samples the same sky function along the reflected ray, so the reflection matches the sky overhead.\n- **Animation and camera:** a `waveClock` uniform is updated every frame. The camera sits 2.4 m above the water and looks toward the horizon.\n- **Colour output:** the shaders output linear colour. Display colours are converted with the engine's `colorspace_decode` include. I set the app output to `toneMapping: \"neutral\"` with exposure 1, since I couldn't confirm the engine's default tone mapping. The chosen operator and exposure are untested.\n- **Ready flag:** `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set on the second engine frame, with a `requestAnimationFrame` fallback after `app.ready()`.\n\n",
